WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.650 --> 00:00:05.520
- Hello, good evening everyone.

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:06.530
I'm Lynn Cooley,

00:00:06.530 --> 00:00:09.900
the Dean of the Graduate
School of Arts and Sciences.

00:00:09.900 --> 00:00:11.670
I am pleased to welcome you

00:00:11.670 --> 00:00:15.850
to our second presentation
in a series of conversations

00:00:15.850 --> 00:00:18.340
with Yale graduate school alumni

00:00:18.340 --> 00:00:20.120
sponsored by the Graduate School

00:00:20.120 --> 00:00:22.483
and its wonderful alumni board.

00:00:23.550 --> 00:00:25.993
This series called Perspectives,

00:00:26.910 --> 00:00:30.500
is meant to bridge our alumni
and student communities,

00:00:30.500 --> 00:00:32.400
sharing alumni experiences

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:35.410
and insights as both models for success,

00:00:35.410 --> 00:00:38.110
and for opening opportunities for alumni

00:00:38.110 --> 00:00:39.843
and students to connect.

00:00:40.860 --> 00:00:43.220
I have ambitious goals for transforming

00:00:43.220 --> 00:00:46.750
and evolving Yale's approach
to doctoral education,

00:00:46.750 --> 00:00:48.620
as well as plans to strengthen

00:00:48.620 --> 00:00:50.980
and build our alumni community,

00:00:50.980 --> 00:00:53.570
to connect our faculty, administrators,

00:00:53.570 --> 00:00:55.810
and students more frequently,

00:00:55.810 --> 00:00:57.660
and to help ignite those connections

00:00:57.660 --> 00:00:59.713
in support of our students.

00:01:00.650 --> 00:01:03.260
I look forward to using what
we learned from this series

00:01:03.260 --> 00:01:05.170
to think about how we can improve,

00:01:05.170 --> 00:01:08.380
continue to improve
graduate student experiences

00:01:08.380 --> 00:01:10.233
for all students at Yale.

00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:14.110
Today, tonight, we will be joined

00:01:14.110 --> 00:01:17.250
by Lyndsey McMillon-Brown, PhD,

00:01:17.250 --> 00:01:19.450
alumni from the Department of Chemical

00:01:19.450 --> 00:01:21.263
and Environmental Engineering.

00:01:22.300 --> 00:01:25.610
Tarek Fadel, PhD, Assistant Director

00:01:25.610 --> 00:01:30.150
of the Marble Center for
Cancer Nanomedicine at MIT

00:01:30.150 --> 00:01:33.160
and our own Danica Tisdale-Fisher,

00:01:33.160 --> 00:01:37.070
Assistant Dean for Diversity
here at the Graduate School.

00:01:37.070 --> 00:01:39.410
We hope that you will
enjoy this conversation

00:01:39.410 --> 00:01:41.410
about Lyndsey's scholarship

00:01:41.410 --> 00:01:43.660
and the lessons she has
learned from her days

00:01:43.660 --> 00:01:46.600
as a graduate student at Yale and beyond.

00:01:46.600 --> 00:01:49.160
I'm delighted to have so
many graduate students

00:01:49.160 --> 00:01:52.210
and alumni and a few
faculty I see on the list,

00:01:52.210 --> 00:01:54.120
tuning in from around the world,

00:01:54.120 --> 00:01:56.863
and I hope you will
enjoy the conversation.

00:01:57.770 --> 00:01:58.603
- Thank you Lynn

00:01:58.603 --> 00:02:00.210
and good evening everyone.

00:02:00.210 --> 00:02:02.120
My name is Danica Tisdale-Fisher,

00:02:02.120 --> 00:02:03.760
and I am Assistant Dean of Diversity

00:02:03.760 --> 00:02:06.270
here at the Yale Graduate School.

00:02:06.270 --> 00:02:07.190
Before we get started,

00:02:07.190 --> 00:02:10.560
I'd like to share a few
housekeeping items with you.

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:12.740
We will be recording this webinar

00:02:12.740 --> 00:02:16.000
and we'll share the recording
after the conversation.

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:19.010
We are offering close
captioning technology,

00:02:19.010 --> 00:02:22.400
which you can access by
clicking the live broadcast link

00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:25.620
in the top left-hand
corner of your screen.

00:02:25.620 --> 00:02:27.440
In the second half of the session,

00:02:27.440 --> 00:02:30.433
we will be saving time
for questions and answers.

00:02:31.570 --> 00:02:33.210
Many graduate students and alumni

00:02:33.210 --> 00:02:35.580
have submitted questions in advance.

00:02:35.580 --> 00:02:38.470
And we also hope you will
enter additional questions

00:02:38.470 --> 00:02:41.810
in the Q&amp;A box during this webinar.

00:02:41.810 --> 00:02:45.833
Now, I am delighted to introduce
Lyndsey McMillon-Brown.

00:02:46.820 --> 00:02:50.280
Dr. McMillon-Brown is a
research electrical engineer

00:02:50.280 --> 00:02:52.680
at NASA Glenn Research Center,

00:02:52.680 --> 00:02:56.370
where she focuses on solar
cell materials development.

00:02:56.370 --> 00:02:58.810
She is currently the
principal investigator

00:02:58.810 --> 00:03:02.880
of $2.5 million early career initiative,

00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:05.170
developing a new class of solar cells

00:03:05.170 --> 00:03:09.030
that can be manufactured
in space and on the moon.

00:03:09.030 --> 00:03:11.130
Prior to joining NASA,

00:03:11.130 --> 00:03:13.770
Lyndsey earned her PhD
in chemical engineering

00:03:13.770 --> 00:03:16.640
at Yale University in 2019,

00:03:16.640 --> 00:03:18.830
where she researched novel materials

00:03:18.830 --> 00:03:19.890
and nano patterns

00:03:19.890 --> 00:03:22.793
for advanced light
trapping in solar cells.

00:03:23.840 --> 00:03:26.010
Outside of the lab, Lyndsey is dedicated

00:03:26.010 --> 00:03:27.420
to increasing opportunities

00:03:27.420 --> 00:03:31.150
for underrepresented
individuals in STEM fields.

00:03:31.150 --> 00:03:34.720
She challenges institutions
to commit to cultural changes

00:03:34.720 --> 00:03:36.870
that provide more supportive environments

00:03:36.870 --> 00:03:38.500
for their students.

00:03:38.500 --> 00:03:41.030
We will be talking about
her experience today

00:03:41.030 --> 00:03:42.180
and her recommendations

00:03:42.180 --> 00:03:45.940
for how research spaces
can be more inclusive.

00:03:45.940 --> 00:03:48.580
Dr. Terek Fadel will
start our conversation

00:03:48.580 --> 00:03:50.660
about transitioning from graduate school

00:03:50.660 --> 00:03:52.573
to a career in the sciences.

00:03:55.340 --> 00:03:56.320
- Thanks so much Danica.

00:03:56.320 --> 00:03:57.620
Can you guys hear me okay?

00:03:58.460 --> 00:04:00.350
It is a pleasure to be here,

00:04:00.350 --> 00:04:01.530
like Lyndsey, I'm a fellow,

00:04:01.530 --> 00:04:03.100
I went to The Yale School of Engineering

00:04:03.100 --> 00:04:04.770
and Applied Science.

00:04:04.770 --> 00:04:06.550
And shortly after a postdoc at Yale,

00:04:06.550 --> 00:04:10.360
I spent, well, it's been what,
10 years now, which is crazy.

00:04:10.360 --> 00:04:13.864
I spent several years in science
policy focusing in a field

00:04:13.864 --> 00:04:17.760
of nanoscience and emerging
technologies in Washington DC.

00:04:17.760 --> 00:04:20.120
And now I manage a research center at MIT

00:04:20.120 --> 00:04:22.070
called the Mobile Center
for Cancer Nanomedicine.

00:04:22.070 --> 00:04:25.600
I'm so excited to
pertain this conversation

00:04:25.600 --> 00:04:29.510
and Lyndsey, what an
amazing professional journey

00:04:29.510 --> 00:04:31.330
you've had so far.

00:04:31.330 --> 00:04:34.990
I really wanted to
start from the beginning

00:04:34.990 --> 00:04:36.550
and ask you a little bit more

00:04:36.550 --> 00:04:39.170
about how you first fell
in love with science

00:04:39.170 --> 00:04:42.193
and how did you ultimately end up in NASA?

00:04:43.670 --> 00:04:45.170
- Well, good evening everyone.

00:04:45.170 --> 00:04:48.520
And I'm so happy to be
here with all of you today,

00:04:48.520 --> 00:04:51.113
and Tarek, that's a great starting point.

00:04:52.190 --> 00:04:56.000
I fell in love with science
from a really early age.

00:04:56.000 --> 00:04:58.380
I was fortunate that both of my parents

00:04:58.380 --> 00:05:01.550
were really enthusiastic about science.

00:05:01.550 --> 00:05:03.950
So I got a lot of hands-on experience

00:05:03.950 --> 00:05:07.740
with my dad outside
tinkering with the cars,

00:05:07.740 --> 00:05:09.480
doing nature walks and hikes

00:05:09.480 --> 00:05:12.210
and gathering, catching
animals that we could.

00:05:12.210 --> 00:05:16.940
And my mom was really, had
the textbooks in order.

00:05:16.940 --> 00:05:18.990
So we were learning things in the house

00:05:18.990 --> 00:05:20.510
and getting those math skills together

00:05:20.510 --> 00:05:22.102
and all those things.

00:05:22.102 --> 00:05:25.310
So that really was a great
introduction to science.

00:05:25.310 --> 00:05:29.770
I went to Space Camp as a
kid, and that was amazing.

00:05:29.770 --> 00:05:31.390
It's a fun experience.

00:05:31.390 --> 00:05:33.630
I would recommend it 10 out of 10.

00:05:33.630 --> 00:05:35.800
So I had a really great time,

00:05:35.800 --> 00:05:37.720
doing different science camps

00:05:37.720 --> 00:05:40.200
and having that type of exposure,

00:05:40.200 --> 00:05:44.090
which is a privilege that
not everyone gets access to,

00:05:44.090 --> 00:05:47.010
but those experiences really did help me

00:05:47.010 --> 00:05:48.710
fall in love with science.

00:05:48.710 --> 00:05:51.140
And then I ended up at NASA

00:05:51.140 --> 00:05:53.860
through more experiences,
doing internships.

00:05:53.860 --> 00:05:56.400
So early in college,

00:05:56.400 --> 00:06:01.160
I applied for a summer
internship opportunity

00:06:01.160 --> 00:06:05.160
and that just so happened to
be in the field of solar cells.

00:06:05.160 --> 00:06:07.950
I was always an environmentalist

00:06:07.950 --> 00:06:10.820
and I thought that I knew solar cells

00:06:10.820 --> 00:06:12.540
were kind of important

00:06:12.540 --> 00:06:14.230
but I had no idea how they worked

00:06:14.230 --> 00:06:16.050
and the science behind them.

00:06:16.050 --> 00:06:18.050
So it was at that first internship

00:06:18.050 --> 00:06:21.217
that I was exposed to
the research behind them

00:06:21.217 --> 00:06:23.490
and the science and the engineering,

00:06:23.490 --> 00:06:24.940
and I loved the technology,

00:06:24.940 --> 00:06:26.670
and I loved how I felt.

00:06:26.670 --> 00:06:28.470
I felt like I was making
a difference in the world

00:06:28.470 --> 00:06:30.900
and really making a positive contribution.

00:06:30.900 --> 00:06:32.620
So I was hooked at that point

00:06:32.620 --> 00:06:36.010
and I continued through NASA as an intern,

00:06:36.010 --> 00:06:38.057
and then I joined their co-op program,

00:06:38.057 --> 00:06:39.230
and I'm happy to say,

00:06:39.230 --> 00:06:41.003
I'm a civil servant there today.

00:06:42.950 --> 00:06:47.950
- It's amazing, a lot of times
I have many conversations,

00:06:48.620 --> 00:06:50.220
with graduate students post-doc,

00:06:50.220 --> 00:06:51.170
a couple of them actually

00:06:51.170 --> 00:06:53.497
in the participant list here tonight.

00:06:53.497 --> 00:06:55.450
And it's remarkable

00:06:55.450 --> 00:06:56.922
because he asked me a lot of times,

00:06:56.922 --> 00:06:58.880
when you think about this journey,

00:06:58.880 --> 00:07:00.240
is it a linear journey

00:07:00.240 --> 00:07:02.810
or are there like many
pivots along the way?

00:07:02.810 --> 00:07:04.810
I see you've done a lot of internships,

00:07:04.810 --> 00:07:07.838
you've been able to kind
of just fall in love

00:07:07.838 --> 00:07:10.170
with the stars, right?

00:07:10.170 --> 00:07:12.580
And think a little bit more
about how to get there.

00:07:12.580 --> 00:07:13.740
And you've had internships through it.

00:07:13.740 --> 00:07:15.803
You went through Space Camp earlier on,

00:07:16.810 --> 00:07:18.400
but did you ever feel like,

00:07:18.400 --> 00:07:20.770
well, maybe I could use solar powers

00:07:20.770 --> 00:07:22.500
and commercial applications.

00:07:22.500 --> 00:07:24.420
Maybe I could shift there,

00:07:24.420 --> 00:07:26.300
was it more of like a
process of elimination

00:07:26.300 --> 00:07:28.460
or was it kind of like, I
know what I'm going to do

00:07:28.460 --> 00:07:29.440
and I'm gonna head there,

00:07:29.440 --> 00:07:32.540
I'm gonna just be bold and go for it.

00:07:32.540 --> 00:07:34.200
- Yeah, I think I was fortunate enough

00:07:34.200 --> 00:07:36.440
to be able to realize the goal

00:07:36.440 --> 00:07:38.650
and decide I would love to do solar panels

00:07:38.650 --> 00:07:40.380
and I'd love to do them at NASA.

00:07:40.380 --> 00:07:43.100
And fortunately those
doors swung open for me.

00:07:43.100 --> 00:07:44.770
So I was able to stay on that path.

00:07:44.770 --> 00:07:47.830
And I did a lot of
exploration along the way,

00:07:47.830 --> 00:07:50.760
but I still kind of had
that end goal in mind.

00:07:50.760 --> 00:07:54.880
So I did some research on
medical devices along the way.

00:07:54.880 --> 00:07:56.930
I was able to work at Johnson and Johnson

00:07:56.930 --> 00:07:59.500
and engineer a bit of medical devices.

00:07:59.500 --> 00:08:01.930
So I had some diverse experiences

00:08:01.930 --> 00:08:03.370
and I've definitely flirted

00:08:03.370 --> 00:08:05.500
with the idea of some other things.

00:08:05.500 --> 00:08:08.190
There's so much, so many fun things to do.

00:08:08.190 --> 00:08:11.070
I was a mechanical engineering
major and undergrad,

00:08:11.070 --> 00:08:13.700
and I was very entranced by cars.

00:08:13.700 --> 00:08:16.577
So I thought I would be in
the automotive industry maybe,

00:08:16.577 --> 00:08:18.940
designing the next generation of cars.

00:08:18.940 --> 00:08:20.900
And then you see that
as an actual engineer,

00:08:20.900 --> 00:08:22.310
you're like working on a screw

00:08:22.310 --> 00:08:24.460
that goes in the left
door panel of the car.

00:08:24.460 --> 00:08:28.660
So that became a little less attractive

00:08:28.660 --> 00:08:32.150
but I was really interested
in a lot of different things,

00:08:32.150 --> 00:08:34.720
but solar was really been the home for me.

00:08:34.720 --> 00:08:37.176
- And it's amazing to hear that.

00:08:37.176 --> 00:08:38.713
You mentioned your parents,

00:08:40.208 --> 00:08:41.130
a lot of these discussions

00:08:41.130 --> 00:08:43.972
about how the tales of pivots

00:08:43.972 --> 00:08:47.200
or jumps from one adventure to another

00:08:47.200 --> 00:08:49.236
in the world of science,

00:08:49.236 --> 00:08:54.150
it can be driven a lot
of times by your passion

00:08:54.150 --> 00:08:58.510
for what you do, what you
tinker with or what you work on,

00:08:58.510 --> 00:09:00.630
but a lot of times it is the engagement

00:09:00.630 --> 00:09:02.190
and we'll get to that later as well,

00:09:02.190 --> 00:09:03.437
but with the people, right?

00:09:03.437 --> 00:09:06.200
And you mentioned at how your parents

00:09:06.200 --> 00:09:07.920
kind of inspired you

00:09:07.920 --> 00:09:09.810
to go through all of the Space Camp,

00:09:09.810 --> 00:09:12.610
had the books stacked up and ready to go.

00:09:12.610 --> 00:09:16.560
But do you feel that that
played a big role as well

00:09:16.560 --> 00:09:19.940
in terms of where you
ended up at the end or...

00:09:19.940 --> 00:09:21.660
- Absolutely, it was a huge role.

00:09:21.660 --> 00:09:23.250
And I think the biggest thing

00:09:23.250 --> 00:09:24.213
that my parents enabled for me

00:09:24.213 --> 00:09:26.810
that I didn't even realize necessarily

00:09:26.810 --> 00:09:29.680
or appreciate until I got much older,

00:09:29.680 --> 00:09:33.720
was that they instilled the skills

00:09:33.720 --> 00:09:37.190
and the belief that I
belonged in any space

00:09:37.190 --> 00:09:38.760
that I was interested in.

00:09:38.760 --> 00:09:40.380
So it was fine

00:09:40.380 --> 00:09:43.000
and they encouraged the fact
that I like to get outside

00:09:43.000 --> 00:09:46.030
and get dirty, there weren't
things for boys versus girls

00:09:46.030 --> 00:09:48.010
and there weren't spaces

00:09:48.010 --> 00:09:49.530
that maybe you don't want to do this.

00:09:49.530 --> 00:09:51.430
They encouraged it and nurtured it

00:09:51.430 --> 00:09:54.900
and gave me those experiences
that they saw excited me.

00:09:54.900 --> 00:09:58.280
So that really allowed
me to view the world

00:09:58.280 --> 00:09:59.577
as endless possibilities,

00:09:59.577 --> 00:10:02.113
and I didn't have any
limitations on myself.

00:10:03.290 --> 00:10:05.400
- That's great, I want to talk more

00:10:05.400 --> 00:10:06.650
about these endless possibilities.

00:10:06.650 --> 00:10:08.243
I want to shift a little bit more

00:10:08.243 --> 00:10:10.830
to think a little bit more.

00:10:10.830 --> 00:10:13.620
Let's continue that in
that realm of pivots

00:10:13.620 --> 00:10:16.163
and as you know more than anyone here,

00:10:16.163 --> 00:10:19.450
often in space, we go from very, very hot,

00:10:19.450 --> 00:10:21.890
to very, very, very cold.

00:10:21.890 --> 00:10:24.050
And I could probably say the same

00:10:24.050 --> 00:10:25.690
about graduate school sometimes

00:10:25.690 --> 00:10:28.150
when it comes to what
you're doing in the lab

00:10:28.150 --> 00:10:29.930
and experiencing successes and failure.

00:10:29.930 --> 00:10:32.040
I remember back in the days,

00:10:32.040 --> 00:10:33.890
if my cell culture got contaminated,

00:10:33.890 --> 00:10:37.070
oh my goodness, it was
the end of the world.

00:10:37.070 --> 00:10:38.800
And then when the experiment worked,

00:10:38.800 --> 00:10:40.200
it's kind of like, right?

00:10:40.200 --> 00:10:42.570
It's just the best feeling in the world.

00:10:42.570 --> 00:10:46.530
And so, Yale is such an amazing place

00:10:46.530 --> 00:10:47.670
and it is so unique,

00:10:47.670 --> 00:10:50.500
and there's such a breadth of resources

00:10:50.500 --> 00:10:51.910
and the people there.

00:10:51.910 --> 00:10:53.960
And I just wanna hear a little bit more

00:10:53.960 --> 00:10:55.500
about how you manage these ups and downs

00:10:55.500 --> 00:10:56.550
while you were there.

00:10:57.670 --> 00:11:01.180
- Yeah, so I took kind of
a multifaceted approach

00:11:01.180 --> 00:11:03.070
to managing those ups and downs.

00:11:03.070 --> 00:11:07.410
And one of the key things
was having a support system

00:11:07.410 --> 00:11:08.900
and a support network.

00:11:08.900 --> 00:11:13.250
So I had the support network of my family

00:11:13.250 --> 00:11:17.220
that was ready-made and always
there with me and for me,

00:11:17.220 --> 00:11:19.740
but then I also had
this new support network

00:11:19.740 --> 00:11:21.070
that I was building at Yale,

00:11:21.070 --> 00:11:23.110
and that consisted of the friends

00:11:23.110 --> 00:11:25.950
that I made organically on campus,

00:11:25.950 --> 00:11:28.560
and they were interdisciplinary
studying different things.

00:11:28.560 --> 00:11:32.150
And we supported each other
through the hot and cold,

00:11:32.150 --> 00:11:33.240
the ups and downs,

00:11:33.240 --> 00:11:36.310
and trials and tribulations of
the graduate school journey.

00:11:36.310 --> 00:11:38.070
And then in many instances,

00:11:38.070 --> 00:11:42.221
there would not necessarily
be the exact support

00:11:42.221 --> 00:11:44.900
that I wanted around.

00:11:44.900 --> 00:11:47.250
And in those instances,

00:11:47.250 --> 00:11:49.840
I would get together
with friends on campus

00:11:49.840 --> 00:11:51.570
and we would build it ourselves.

00:11:51.570 --> 00:11:53.290
And I think that's important to do

00:11:53.290 --> 00:11:57.180
because a lot of times we are in spaces

00:11:57.180 --> 00:12:01.990
that are not yet ready for
people like us to be in.

00:12:01.990 --> 00:12:03.960
So women in STEM

00:12:03.960 --> 00:12:08.960
or people of color in these
tremendously new environments

00:12:09.330 --> 00:12:12.160
that have been historically
under representing people.

00:12:12.160 --> 00:12:14.630
So it's important that we make space

00:12:14.630 --> 00:12:16.510
and claim space that we need

00:12:16.510 --> 00:12:18.720
to support us through these times.

00:12:18.720 --> 00:12:22.130
And for me, that looked
like co-founding the society

00:12:22.130 --> 00:12:23.090
of women engineers,

00:12:23.090 --> 00:12:24.710
with some women in the chemical

00:12:24.710 --> 00:12:27.190
and environmental engineering program,

00:12:27.190 --> 00:12:29.860
that was co-founding the Yale
League of Black Scientists

00:12:29.860 --> 00:12:34.620
with two physicists, Charles
Brown and Brooke Russell.

00:12:34.620 --> 00:12:37.920
And also I found a great home

00:12:37.920 --> 00:12:39.970
at the university church at Yale.

00:12:39.970 --> 00:12:43.070
And that was where I really felt community

00:12:43.070 --> 00:12:44.290
and felt welcome.

00:12:44.290 --> 00:12:47.590
And it wasn't the STEM support

00:12:47.590 --> 00:12:49.870
and it wasn't in my field or discipline,

00:12:49.870 --> 00:12:51.580
but it really fed my soul.

00:12:51.580 --> 00:12:54.289
And we sometimes forget

00:12:54.289 --> 00:12:55.540
that we are whole people

00:12:55.540 --> 00:12:58.620
and I needed to nourish my whole self.

00:12:58.620 --> 00:13:00.950
So I was able to kind of
build that support network

00:13:00.950 --> 00:13:03.630
across campus in many different places.

00:13:03.630 --> 00:13:04.540
- I love that.

00:13:04.540 --> 00:13:06.790
And nourishing your
whole self is definitely,

00:13:10.049 --> 00:13:12.760
just a recurring kind of quality that feel

00:13:12.760 --> 00:13:16.320
that all of us kind of need
throughout this journey

00:13:16.320 --> 00:13:17.670
in graduate school and beyond.

00:13:17.670 --> 00:13:19.450
And it's just remarkable

00:13:19.450 --> 00:13:22.900
how much of a moment when
certainty can be when,

00:13:22.900 --> 00:13:24.570
you feel like you're dwelling yourself

00:13:24.570 --> 00:13:26.700
into a subject of research,

00:13:26.700 --> 00:13:28.070
whether it's in the sciences or beyond,

00:13:28.070 --> 00:13:29.140
and a lot of the,

00:13:29.140 --> 00:13:31.550
it seems like a lot of
kind of your existence,

00:13:31.550 --> 00:13:32.730
kind of revolves around it.

00:13:32.730 --> 00:13:34.640
And it's just, it's great.

00:13:34.640 --> 00:13:37.700
and I wanted to kind of just remember

00:13:37.700 --> 00:13:40.790
how enabling that landscape on campus was,

00:13:40.790 --> 00:13:43.380
but also to not just forget about,

00:13:43.380 --> 00:13:46.080
I mean, you started a SWEE chapter, right?

00:13:46.080 --> 00:13:46.913
Can you tell somebody,

00:13:46.913 --> 00:13:49.480
well, I mean, I love how you go,

00:13:49.480 --> 00:13:51.430
but really, it was amazing

00:13:51.430 --> 00:13:53.660
and it took a lot of efforts

00:13:53.660 --> 00:13:56.100
and just being able to convince yourself

00:13:56.100 --> 00:13:57.990
and then seeing around you,

00:13:57.990 --> 00:13:58.910
and just being like, you know what?

00:13:58.910 --> 00:13:59.743
We need this,

00:13:59.743 --> 00:14:00.710
and can you tell us a bit more,

00:14:00.710 --> 00:14:02.030
how did that happen?

00:14:02.030 --> 00:14:05.370
- Yeah, so that was
definitely a proud moment

00:14:05.370 --> 00:14:06.380
in grad school.

00:14:06.380 --> 00:14:10.550
And that came from a group
of us women grad students,

00:14:10.550 --> 00:14:13.640
sitting in Mason lab at lunch,

00:14:13.640 --> 00:14:17.150
talking about, well, at
my undergrad, we had this

00:14:17.150 --> 00:14:18.550
and my undergrad had this,

00:14:18.550 --> 00:14:20.480
and that's what we need around here.

00:14:20.480 --> 00:14:22.700
Gripe, gripe, grumble, grumble.

00:14:22.700 --> 00:14:24.357
And finally we had kicked it around enough

00:14:24.357 --> 00:14:27.450
and we were actually telling me undergrads

00:14:27.450 --> 00:14:29.883
that were also in the
engineering departments,

00:14:30.830 --> 00:14:32.260
telling them these tall tales

00:14:32.260 --> 00:14:34.210
of what our SWEE chapters used to do

00:14:34.210 --> 00:14:35.880
and how we felt community there,

00:14:35.880 --> 00:14:38.600
and what a rich experience this was,

00:14:38.600 --> 00:14:40.390
how you're able to host girl scout days

00:14:40.390 --> 00:14:42.720
and reach out to local girl scout troops

00:14:42.720 --> 00:14:44.730
to inspire the next generation.

00:14:44.730 --> 00:14:47.360
And there were enough of us around

00:14:47.360 --> 00:14:48.610
and excited about it.

00:14:48.610 --> 00:14:49.443
And I think it was something

00:14:49.443 --> 00:14:52.780
that we really wanted to
give to the undergrads

00:14:52.780 --> 00:14:55.240
so that they could have the
same experience and support

00:14:55.240 --> 00:14:57.940
and love for the organization that we did.

00:14:57.940 --> 00:15:00.427
So we were led by Bridget Heggerty

00:15:00.427 --> 00:15:03.590
and we were able to found this chapter,

00:15:03.590 --> 00:15:05.250
get it recognized.

00:15:05.250 --> 00:15:06.510
It began as the grad chapter,

00:15:06.510 --> 00:15:08.700
but there's a thriving undergrad chapter

00:15:08.700 --> 00:15:10.380
and it's still there.

00:15:10.380 --> 00:15:14.480
And women are still benefiting
from that chapter at Yale.

00:15:14.480 --> 00:15:18.590
And we go to the annual
conference each year,

00:15:18.590 --> 00:15:21.220
which has been tremendously valuable

00:15:21.220 --> 00:15:23.060
for the undergrads seeking employment.

00:15:23.060 --> 00:15:25.500
They have a great job fair at
the SWEE Annual Conference.

00:15:25.500 --> 00:15:29.200
So it's kind of the, it
nurtured us in the moment

00:15:29.200 --> 00:15:30.033
when we founded it.

00:15:30.033 --> 00:15:32.700
And it gave us something
to do that we believed in,

00:15:32.700 --> 00:15:34.470
but it's still paying off so well

00:15:34.470 --> 00:15:36.040
for the future generations.

00:15:36.040 --> 00:15:38.900
And we're proud of that.

00:15:38.900 --> 00:15:40.180
- That's amazing.

00:15:40.180 --> 00:15:42.050
Yeah, it's phenomenal.

00:15:42.050 --> 00:15:44.890
I feel like when you start these types

00:15:44.890 --> 00:15:46.310
of new initiatives and efforts

00:15:46.310 --> 00:15:47.327
and provide this new support structure,

00:15:47.327 --> 00:15:50.120
you always wonder, how
is this sustainable?

00:15:50.120 --> 00:15:53.670
And it feels amazing to just to know

00:15:53.670 --> 00:15:54.520
that it still exists

00:15:54.520 --> 00:15:55.500
and it's still going.

00:15:55.500 --> 00:15:57.350
And that's awesome.

00:15:57.350 --> 00:15:59.590
I wanted to shift gear
a little bit more now,

00:15:59.590 --> 00:16:03.100
about being a NASA Glenn

00:16:03.100 --> 00:16:06.710
and how you're leading
the study of solar cells.

00:16:06.710 --> 00:16:09.640
So this is part of the
Artemis Program, right?

00:16:09.640 --> 00:16:10.473
- Correct.

00:16:11.510 --> 00:16:14.630
- So you providing
astronauts new superpowers

00:16:14.630 --> 00:16:17.080
and what I mean by that solar power.

00:16:17.080 --> 00:16:19.300
And so how did you make the transition

00:16:19.300 --> 00:16:21.470
from being in graduate school?

00:16:21.470 --> 00:16:23.060
So we talked about internships and all,

00:16:23.060 --> 00:16:25.010
but it's remarkable

00:16:25.010 --> 00:16:28.775
because when you transitioned
into this new workplace

00:16:28.775 --> 00:16:31.440
and you have to adapt, right?

00:16:31.440 --> 00:16:34.600
I mean, we're talking
about accountability,

00:16:34.600 --> 00:16:37.320
resources are changing,
your workspace is changing,

00:16:37.320 --> 00:16:38.870
the people you're engaged with,

00:16:39.730 --> 00:16:41.170
what are the things you had to learn

00:16:41.170 --> 00:16:42.853
on your own in this journey?

00:16:44.030 --> 00:16:46.623
- Yeah, so I had to learn a lot,

00:16:47.520 --> 00:16:52.520
but I did come in after
the experience of the PhD

00:16:52.620 --> 00:16:57.620
with some comfort of the
research and the science

00:16:58.200 --> 00:17:02.690
and understanding how to
tackle a large problem.

00:17:02.690 --> 00:17:05.160
So those were some of the
tools that I had already had,

00:17:05.160 --> 00:17:08.250
but one thing I've really had to learn,

00:17:08.250 --> 00:17:12.800
is how do you nurture and
manage the people on your team?

00:17:12.800 --> 00:17:14.800
And people are really important to me.

00:17:14.800 --> 00:17:17.730
So I wanted to do this with tact.

00:17:17.730 --> 00:17:19.550
When do you push

00:17:19.550 --> 00:17:22.310
and when do you give
grace and accommodate?

00:17:22.310 --> 00:17:24.810
And I don't know that that's
something that can be taught,

00:17:24.810 --> 00:17:27.900
but it is something
that I think is critical

00:17:27.900 --> 00:17:29.990
no matter what we do.

00:17:29.990 --> 00:17:32.360
Then there are things about
running a research group

00:17:32.360 --> 00:17:34.900
like how do you initiate collaborations

00:17:34.900 --> 00:17:36.880
with researchers that
you want to work with,

00:17:36.880 --> 00:17:39.240
but who you don't know personally yet,

00:17:39.240 --> 00:17:41.830
who you don't have a relationship with?

00:17:41.830 --> 00:17:43.500
And then the administrative tax,

00:17:43.500 --> 00:17:46.160
man, they get me every day.

00:17:46.160 --> 00:17:48.487
That's the one thing I'm
always griping about.

00:17:48.487 --> 00:17:51.730
And those tasks have been
greater than I anticipated.

00:17:51.730 --> 00:17:54.480
I envisioned the life as a PI

00:17:54.480 --> 00:17:58.200
where I was really still in the lab,

00:17:58.200 --> 00:17:59.750
but I have to fight for that time

00:17:59.750 --> 00:18:03.640
because now I have to keep the
supplies for everybody else,

00:18:03.640 --> 00:18:05.590
I have to make sure the
contracts are up to date.

00:18:05.590 --> 00:18:07.210
So the postdocs are getting paid

00:18:07.210 --> 00:18:08.890
and safety permits,

00:18:08.890 --> 00:18:10.980
and meetings with program managers,

00:18:10.980 --> 00:18:12.860
and you have to get the money

00:18:12.860 --> 00:18:14.870
and then drum up support for more money

00:18:14.870 --> 00:18:16.200
and publish the papers,

00:18:16.200 --> 00:18:18.830
and traveling to give talks around

00:18:18.830 --> 00:18:21.170
at different labs and universities.

00:18:21.170 --> 00:18:24.520
So it's been a lot to handle,

00:18:24.520 --> 00:18:27.920
but I've been fortunate to have mentors

00:18:27.920 --> 00:18:31.530
and people around that
can give me that advice.

00:18:31.530 --> 00:18:33.450
I think it's important to try to identify

00:18:33.450 --> 00:18:37.030
what you don't know so that
you can get help with it.

00:18:37.030 --> 00:18:39.680
So yeah, leveraging relationships
has been really important

00:18:39.680 --> 00:18:41.463
in this transition time for me.

00:18:42.310 --> 00:18:45.050
- Yeah, I'm hearing a
lot of great things here.

00:18:45.050 --> 00:18:49.560
I mean, first of all, the
reality of a private investigator

00:18:49.560 --> 00:18:51.130
and into sciences, right?

00:18:51.130 --> 00:18:55.890
And how this new reality is
really wearing many hats,

00:18:55.890 --> 00:19:00.890
learning how to manage your
budget, advocating, policy,

00:19:01.010 --> 00:19:02.770
managing people, conflict,

00:19:02.770 --> 00:19:05.970
not just what's happening on
the bench or projects, right?

00:19:05.970 --> 00:19:07.950
And I feel like it's a new reality

00:19:07.950 --> 00:19:11.430
that is not just, in your
setting for instance,

00:19:11.430 --> 00:19:13.380
but also in academia.

00:19:13.380 --> 00:19:16.140
And I want it to just kind of go back

00:19:16.140 --> 00:19:18.290
and think about all the great
things you've done on campus.

00:19:18.290 --> 00:19:20.629
When were at Yale, you actually,

00:19:20.629 --> 00:19:23.380
you did the diligence effort

00:19:23.380 --> 00:19:25.770
to actually get flavors of that,

00:19:25.770 --> 00:19:28.840
through the Advanced Graduate
Leadership Fellow Program,

00:19:28.840 --> 00:19:30.610
through some of the governance structures,

00:19:30.610 --> 00:19:32.683
you've even stored through SWEE.

00:19:33.590 --> 00:19:38.272
I feel like there's definitely
some sort of linkage, right?

00:19:38.272 --> 00:19:40.960
But ultimately I think that
the one take home there

00:19:40.960 --> 00:19:44.290
is that science is not that simple

00:19:44.290 --> 00:19:46.910
to begin with on the
scientific end for sure

00:19:46.910 --> 00:19:49.010
but also in the human aspect of it

00:19:49.010 --> 00:19:50.380
and (indistinct) aspect of it.

00:19:50.380 --> 00:19:51.470
And then like the menu has

00:19:51.470 --> 00:19:53.760
that we have to wear nowadays.

00:19:53.760 --> 00:19:55.630
Yeah, no, it's remarkable.

00:19:55.630 --> 00:19:57.120
Do you have any comments though,

00:19:57.120 --> 00:20:00.490
in terms of how your experience of depth

00:20:00.490 --> 00:20:02.350
and breadth for Yale engineering

00:20:02.350 --> 00:20:07.350
has helped you kind of
just provide this affinity

00:20:07.520 --> 00:20:10.630
to do all these different
things you're doing now in NASA?

00:20:10.630 --> 00:20:12.250
- Yes, it's helped tremendously.

00:20:12.250 --> 00:20:16.980
So I realized I was in a really
good position technically

00:20:16.980 --> 00:20:18.837
because I had experience writing papers.

00:20:18.837 --> 00:20:22.870
And also as a grad student, I
had experience writing grants.

00:20:22.870 --> 00:20:24.840
So that was great to come in with that,

00:20:24.840 --> 00:20:26.280
like that experience

00:20:26.280 --> 00:20:28.870
and that hardware to be able to do that.

00:20:28.870 --> 00:20:30.685
And then as you mentioned,

00:20:30.685 --> 00:20:33.820
you kind of build up
this toolbox of skills,

00:20:33.820 --> 00:20:36.860
transferable skills
that as a grad student,

00:20:36.860 --> 00:20:39.960
they were fun pastimes and activities,

00:20:39.960 --> 00:20:42.520
but then you realize,
oh, I'm able to do this

00:20:42.520 --> 00:20:44.460
because of these previous experiences.

00:20:44.460 --> 00:20:48.590
So from being a resident coordinator

00:20:48.590 --> 00:20:49.800
over the graduate housing,

00:20:49.800 --> 00:20:52.980
I had experience running a large team

00:20:52.980 --> 00:20:55.380
and having a fairly large budget

00:20:55.380 --> 00:20:57.090
with goals and milestones,

00:20:57.090 --> 00:20:58.960
and making sure those were met

00:20:58.960 --> 00:21:01.170
to satisfy the needs of a community.

00:21:01.170 --> 00:21:04.780
I had a lot of experience
working with diverse individuals.

00:21:04.780 --> 00:21:08.410
I had also as a resident coordinator,

00:21:08.410 --> 00:21:13.410
I learned a lot about different
marginalized identities

00:21:13.470 --> 00:21:16.210
and what challenges
other people might face.

00:21:16.210 --> 00:21:20.010
And by having to go to the
different resources on campus,

00:21:20.010 --> 00:21:22.230
I learned a lot about people.

00:21:22.230 --> 00:21:24.030
So I'm able to implement that

00:21:24.030 --> 00:21:26.490
and how do we diversify STEM,

00:21:26.490 --> 00:21:28.870
and how to, I make sure that all people

00:21:28.870 --> 00:21:30.840
of all identities feel welcome, seen

00:21:30.840 --> 00:21:34.490
and heard in any space
that I'm sharing with them.

00:21:34.490 --> 00:21:36.462
So there were so many skills

00:21:36.462 --> 00:21:38.950
that I was able to learn and transfer.

00:21:38.950 --> 00:21:42.283
So I'm really grateful
for that experience.

00:21:42.283 --> 00:21:46.484
And then there's just
the diversity of thought

00:21:46.484 --> 00:21:48.680
that is the Yale experience

00:21:48.680 --> 00:21:51.630
and that has enriched
my life tremendously.

00:21:51.630 --> 00:21:54.300
So I had friends in grad school

00:21:54.300 --> 00:21:55.660
that were in the humanities

00:21:55.660 --> 00:21:57.970
and doing PhDs in different disciplines.

00:21:57.970 --> 00:21:59.780
And just from having lunch with them

00:21:59.780 --> 00:22:01.810
or catching up, you hear
what they're reading,

00:22:01.810 --> 00:22:03.150
what they're writing about.

00:22:03.150 --> 00:22:05.720
And that has enriched
me so much as a person.

00:22:05.720 --> 00:22:07.930
If you probably were to
look at my bookshelf,

00:22:07.930 --> 00:22:10.140
you would think I was
in like the humanities

00:22:10.140 --> 00:22:11.120
and not a scientist

00:22:11.120 --> 00:22:13.210
because I find their work so interesting.

00:22:13.210 --> 00:22:17.750
So they kind of ignite this
whole other side of my brain

00:22:17.750 --> 00:22:19.830
and I'm grateful to have
a better understanding

00:22:19.830 --> 00:22:21.270
of the world that we live in.

00:22:21.270 --> 00:22:23.980
And that was absolutely acquired from Yale

00:22:23.980 --> 00:22:25.380
and from those diverse people

00:22:25.380 --> 00:22:27.173
that I met with different studies.

00:22:28.530 --> 00:22:29.363
- Thank you so much.

00:22:29.363 --> 00:22:30.440
Yeah, we talked a little bit about that.

00:22:30.440 --> 00:22:32.508
Actually my recollection,

00:22:32.508 --> 00:22:35.810
my favorite moments really
were the first Friday of five,

00:22:35.810 --> 00:22:39.090
where I would just go
and mingle with people

00:22:39.090 --> 00:22:41.650
in international relations
and in humanities.

00:22:41.650 --> 00:22:42.857
That was my favorite
part, people were like,

00:22:42.857 --> 00:22:44.458
"Oh, this is great,

00:22:44.458 --> 00:22:46.360
you're doing the biomedical engineering

00:22:46.360 --> 00:22:47.530
or you're so close to the med school."

00:22:47.530 --> 00:22:49.757
I'm like, "No, I'm actually
close to the theater."

00:22:51.220 --> 00:22:53.005
That's where I want to go (laughs).

00:22:53.005 --> 00:22:54.410
- Yes.

00:22:54.410 --> 00:22:58.050
- So anyway, thank you
so much, this is great.

00:22:58.050 --> 00:22:59.790
I want to yield back to Danica

00:22:59.790 --> 00:23:01.760
who has also a lot of questions to ask.

00:23:01.760 --> 00:23:03.356
Thank you so much.

00:23:03.356 --> 00:23:04.500
- [Lyndsey] Thank you.

00:23:04.500 --> 00:23:05.333
- Thank you Tarek

00:23:05.333 --> 00:23:07.040
and Lyndsey, I've been so inspired

00:23:07.040 --> 00:23:10.760
to hear you talk more about how you work

00:23:10.760 --> 00:23:11.730
to create the community

00:23:11.730 --> 00:23:14.620
that you believed you
could thrive in at Yale.

00:23:14.620 --> 00:23:16.170
And so I wanted to ask you

00:23:16.170 --> 00:23:18.270
sort of based on your many experiences

00:23:18.270 --> 00:23:20.310
in STEM outreach and mentoring,

00:23:20.310 --> 00:23:23.310
especially as a member of SWEE and YLBS

00:23:23.310 --> 00:23:25.590
and other groups while you were at Yale.

00:23:25.590 --> 00:23:27.520
What are your thoughts about how students

00:23:27.520 --> 00:23:29.010
or recent graduates

00:23:29.010 --> 00:23:31.940
from underrepresented
communities especially,

00:23:31.940 --> 00:23:33.990
can navigate through today's STEM climate

00:23:33.990 --> 00:23:37.250
both academically or professionally?

00:23:37.250 --> 00:23:39.290
- Yeah, that's a great question.

00:23:39.290 --> 00:23:44.230
So I think, there are a
lot of solutions to that,

00:23:44.230 --> 00:23:46.030
and I think it's really important

00:23:46.030 --> 00:23:50.130
that you find a space

00:23:50.130 --> 00:23:52.630
where you can be your authentic self,

00:23:52.630 --> 00:23:54.840
be sure to bring your authentic self

00:23:54.840 --> 00:23:56.890
and don't sacrifice.

00:23:56.890 --> 00:23:58.963
I think a lot of people have,

00:24:00.840 --> 00:24:02.870
there can be external pressure

00:24:02.870 --> 00:24:05.560
to be a little bit less you,

00:24:05.560 --> 00:24:07.130
whatever makes you, you.

00:24:07.130 --> 00:24:09.040
So that you fit in with the status quo

00:24:09.040 --> 00:24:12.137
of what people may incorrectly think,

00:24:12.137 --> 00:24:15.940
is representative of the field
that you aspire to be in.

00:24:15.940 --> 00:24:18.700
But I always argue for the opposite.

00:24:18.700 --> 00:24:20.210
I think it's tremendously important

00:24:20.210 --> 00:24:21.820
to be uniquely you

00:24:21.820 --> 00:24:24.770
because I think that broadens the access

00:24:24.770 --> 00:24:28.250
to your field for many
other people than just you.

00:24:28.250 --> 00:24:31.740
So I think it's important,
I am a scientist,

00:24:31.740 --> 00:24:35.400
I am a woman, I love
football, I like makeup.

00:24:35.400 --> 00:24:38.380
And I think bringing all of those things

00:24:38.380 --> 00:24:41.350
will help someone see, you
don't have to be a nerd.

00:24:41.350 --> 00:24:43.670
You can watch, Sunday Night Football

00:24:43.670 --> 00:24:45.230
and still do this whole science thing,

00:24:45.230 --> 00:24:46.550
and you can paint your nails

00:24:46.550 --> 00:24:47.720
and still put on the lab coat

00:24:47.720 --> 00:24:49.130
and get in the lab.

00:24:49.130 --> 00:24:53.230
So I think it's important
to be your authentic self,

00:24:53.230 --> 00:24:55.630
find community and make community,

00:24:55.630 --> 00:24:58.180
and be community for other people.

00:24:58.180 --> 00:25:01.980
Community isn't always found
where you would like it to be

00:25:01.980 --> 00:25:04.540
or where it should formally be.

00:25:04.540 --> 00:25:06.760
So if you're not finding the support

00:25:06.760 --> 00:25:08.960
that you would like in your department,

00:25:08.960 --> 00:25:10.456
get outside, swing the doors open.

00:25:10.456 --> 00:25:12.680
And if you're a scientist

00:25:12.680 --> 00:25:14.660
explore other departments in the hill

00:25:14.660 --> 00:25:16.190
and see if you can find people,

00:25:16.190 --> 00:25:18.660
maybe your support system is
going to be in the med school

00:25:18.660 --> 00:25:19.840
or in the law school

00:25:19.840 --> 00:25:21.250
or with people around.

00:25:21.250 --> 00:25:23.490
For me, the university
church was a great space

00:25:23.490 --> 00:25:25.050
for me to find that support.

00:25:25.050 --> 00:25:28.498
And then always I was relying on people

00:25:28.498 --> 00:25:30.810
who had been through
what I'd been through.

00:25:30.810 --> 00:25:33.380
I found mentors that were in positions

00:25:33.380 --> 00:25:35.110
where I would like to be,

00:25:35.110 --> 00:25:37.060
but I never want to overlook the wisdom

00:25:37.060 --> 00:25:39.480
of people that are not in your field,

00:25:39.480 --> 00:25:41.470
but still have kind of navigated

00:25:41.470 --> 00:25:43.580
through the world that we're in today.

00:25:43.580 --> 00:25:46.110
So I think you can learn a great deal

00:25:46.110 --> 00:25:47.640
from so many people.

00:25:47.640 --> 00:25:51.750
And find advocates, people
who advocate for you.

00:25:51.750 --> 00:25:53.060
And an advocate is someone

00:25:53.060 --> 00:25:58.060
who will speak your name into
rooms that you are not there.

00:25:58.430 --> 00:26:00.130
And when opportunities come up,

00:26:00.130 --> 00:26:02.173
when awards are being discussed,

00:26:03.820 --> 00:26:06.880
positions that will help
elevate you to the next level.

00:26:06.880 --> 00:26:08.690
And that can be difficult to find

00:26:08.690 --> 00:26:11.823
but it's important to try
to find those advocates.

00:26:13.020 --> 00:26:14.087
- Thank you for that.

00:26:14.087 --> 00:26:16.663
I love this idea of sort of
busting the myth wide open

00:26:16.663 --> 00:26:18.390
about what a scientist

00:26:18.390 --> 00:26:20.583
or what a scholar should
look like and be like,

00:26:21.776 --> 00:26:23.500
and bringing your most authentic self

00:26:23.500 --> 00:26:24.650
to all of these spaces.

00:26:24.650 --> 00:26:25.890
I think in the work that I do,

00:26:25.890 --> 00:26:28.720
I see that that's a question
many students often have,

00:26:28.720 --> 00:26:31.050
is how real can I be in these spaces?

00:26:31.050 --> 00:26:33.390
And so I love that you're
offering the advice

00:26:33.390 --> 00:26:35.303
to be your most authentic self.

00:26:36.240 --> 00:26:38.090
Just to pivot just a bit though,

00:26:38.090 --> 00:26:39.130
I do want to ask

00:26:39.130 --> 00:26:42.900
about gender diversity in
science and engineering.

00:26:42.900 --> 00:26:45.420
We often talk about a leaky pipeline

00:26:45.420 --> 00:26:48.680
referring to sort of the
decreasing proportion of women

00:26:48.680 --> 00:26:51.950
and underrepresented minorities in STEM.

00:26:51.950 --> 00:26:55.370
I want to ask you what you
suggest graduate schools can do

00:26:55.370 --> 00:26:56.640
to not only attract,

00:26:56.640 --> 00:26:59.823
but also to retain new
generations of scientists.

00:27:00.890 --> 00:27:02.840
- So I have been inspired

00:27:02.840 --> 00:27:06.680
to push back on the concept
of the leaky pipeline,

00:27:06.680 --> 00:27:08.650
because when we talk
about a leaky pipeline,

00:27:08.650 --> 00:27:10.880
that's suggesting a passive loss,

00:27:10.880 --> 00:27:12.020
but in reality,

00:27:12.020 --> 00:27:14.630
the pipeline that we exist in has valves

00:27:14.630 --> 00:27:17.130
and they're restricting
access to some people

00:27:17.130 --> 00:27:18.260
based on their identities.

00:27:18.260 --> 00:27:21.060
And those identities can
be race, sexuality, gender,

00:27:21.060 --> 00:27:23.870
physical ability, myriad things.

00:27:23.870 --> 00:27:27.750
So how do we fix this pipeline?

00:27:27.750 --> 00:27:31.200
How do we stop this restriction of access?

00:27:31.200 --> 00:27:34.740
So I think it's really important

00:27:34.740 --> 00:27:37.480
to make underrepresented students

00:27:37.480 --> 00:27:40.560
or team members feel seen all of the time

00:27:40.560 --> 00:27:42.450
and create an environment
that is supportive

00:27:42.450 --> 00:27:44.400
and collaborative for them.

00:27:44.400 --> 00:27:46.400
Everyone should be encouraged to speak

00:27:46.400 --> 00:27:49.520
and that they have an
equal voice in the team.

00:27:49.520 --> 00:27:52.820
I think the graduate
school and all programs

00:27:52.820 --> 00:27:56.740
should implement holistic
admissions processes

00:27:56.740 --> 00:27:59.270
that focused more on research potential

00:27:59.270 --> 00:28:01.180
by like qualitative assessments

00:28:01.180 --> 00:28:04.490
and de-emphasize metrics
like the GRE and GPA

00:28:04.490 --> 00:28:05.950
that are actually been proven

00:28:05.950 --> 00:28:07.700
to be ineffective predictors

00:28:07.700 --> 00:28:11.460
for research performance
and degree completion.

00:28:11.460 --> 00:28:14.190
Research has shown that
letters of recommendation

00:28:14.190 --> 00:28:16.710
are the strongest predictor for success

00:28:16.710 --> 00:28:18.543
in graduate school programs.

00:28:19.460 --> 00:28:21.150
So I also think we should make

00:28:21.150 --> 00:28:24.700
academic faculty candidate
lists that are diverse.

00:28:24.700 --> 00:28:26.660
We need to diversify the faculty

00:28:26.660 --> 00:28:29.480
because that's going
to aid in the retention

00:28:29.480 --> 00:28:32.780
and recruitment of diverse students.

00:28:32.780 --> 00:28:35.180
We need to make sure that
we're taking serious looks

00:28:35.180 --> 00:28:36.630
at these diverse candidates

00:28:36.630 --> 00:28:38.130
and deans and department chairs

00:28:38.130 --> 00:28:41.253
should be rejecting shortlist
that lacked diversity.

00:28:42.381 --> 00:28:44.540
I would also encourage you that you hire

00:28:44.540 --> 00:28:47.480
or recruit your diverse
candidates and clusters

00:28:47.480 --> 00:28:49.670
so that these candidates have cohort.

00:28:49.670 --> 00:28:52.520
They also need support and community,

00:28:52.520 --> 00:28:54.760
so that they can give
so much of themselves

00:28:54.760 --> 00:28:57.290
to the department, the
school, their scholarship,

00:28:57.290 --> 00:29:00.370
and the students that will
inevitably flop to them

00:29:00.370 --> 00:29:02.190
for support and mentorship.

00:29:02.190 --> 00:29:04.750
So really the bottom line here is that,

00:29:04.750 --> 00:29:05.710
you need to ensure

00:29:05.710 --> 00:29:08.810
that your organization
doesn't have hidden structures

00:29:08.810 --> 00:29:11.350
like leadership, policy and culture,

00:29:11.350 --> 00:29:13.360
that are in opposition to the mission

00:29:13.360 --> 00:29:15.810
of diversity, equity and inclusion.

00:29:15.810 --> 00:29:18.870
- I love that, I really
feel compelled by this idea

00:29:18.870 --> 00:29:21.850
of sort of a comprehensive approach

00:29:21.850 --> 00:29:26.720
to providing pathways, if you will,

00:29:26.720 --> 00:29:28.390
for people to have support

00:29:28.390 --> 00:29:29.750
and community in these spaces,

00:29:29.750 --> 00:29:30.840
at not just the student level,

00:29:30.840 --> 00:29:32.200
but also at the faculty level.

00:29:32.200 --> 00:29:33.400
The idea of cluster hires,

00:29:33.400 --> 00:29:34.430
I think is really important

00:29:34.430 --> 00:29:37.050
especially in the STEM fields,

00:29:37.050 --> 00:29:41.030
but you talked a little bit
about mentors in your life

00:29:41.030 --> 00:29:43.750
who sort of helped you
get to where you are.

00:29:43.750 --> 00:29:45.253
You talked about your parents.

00:29:46.350 --> 00:29:48.580
I wanna dig a little bit deeper

00:29:48.580 --> 00:29:53.400
and find out more about how
those mentorships have shaped,

00:29:53.400 --> 00:29:54.530
who you are now

00:29:54.530 --> 00:29:57.340
and how you pay that forward in your life

00:29:57.340 --> 00:29:58.233
and in your work.

00:29:59.190 --> 00:30:01.020
- Yeah, I believe that mentoring

00:30:01.020 --> 00:30:04.400
is the single most
important thing that we do.

00:30:04.400 --> 00:30:07.410
I think it's a
responsibility for all of us,

00:30:07.410 --> 00:30:09.820
and I've been blessed
to have many mentors.

00:30:09.820 --> 00:30:12.020
And a lot of times I have many mentors

00:30:12.020 --> 00:30:17.020
at the same time working,
serving different roles for me.

00:30:17.040 --> 00:30:18.597
So my parents were my first mentors

00:30:18.597 --> 00:30:20.990
and they're still my mentors today.

00:30:20.990 --> 00:30:24.170
And from them, they
instilled values within me

00:30:24.170 --> 00:30:25.890
to value people over money,

00:30:25.890 --> 00:30:28.980
to be kind, to love the environment.

00:30:28.980 --> 00:30:31.980
I had a wonderful mentor in college,

00:30:31.980 --> 00:30:33.400
Professor Osama (indistinct)

00:30:33.400 --> 00:30:35.370
and he introduced me to research

00:30:35.370 --> 00:30:37.640
and he'd nurtured my curiosity.

00:30:37.640 --> 00:30:39.540
And he mentioned me until he passed away,

00:30:39.540 --> 00:30:40.790
even through graduate school,

00:30:40.790 --> 00:30:43.930
he was a touch point
encouraging me and directing me

00:30:43.930 --> 00:30:45.660
cause he had been through that experience.

00:30:45.660 --> 00:30:48.460
So he knew what that journey was like.

00:30:48.460 --> 00:30:50.620
And then Professor Michael Loewenberg

00:30:50.620 --> 00:30:53.100
was a great mentor for me in grad school.

00:30:53.100 --> 00:30:56.620
He was tremendously supportive,
encouraging, present,

00:30:56.620 --> 00:30:58.530
and a great listener.

00:30:58.530 --> 00:31:01.460
And then at NASA, I also
have wonderful mentors

00:31:01.460 --> 00:31:03.220
and it's been great

00:31:03.220 --> 00:31:06.070
because they knew me
when I was in college.

00:31:06.070 --> 00:31:09.199
So they've been with me
for like 10 years now.

00:31:09.199 --> 00:31:12.600
And they are great mentors

00:31:12.600 --> 00:31:15.530
because they first asked what my goals are

00:31:15.530 --> 00:31:17.080
and what I would like to be,

00:31:17.080 --> 00:31:20.462
even if that journey isn't at NASA.

00:31:20.462 --> 00:31:22.940
Really what do I aspire to do?

00:31:22.940 --> 00:31:25.320
And then they're supplying
me with the tools

00:31:25.320 --> 00:31:28.680
and guidance to help me
get from point A to B.

00:31:28.680 --> 00:31:30.840
So how I pay it forward,

00:31:30.840 --> 00:31:34.680
is I perform outreach across K-12 levels

00:31:34.680 --> 00:31:36.950
to encourage students to pursue STEM

00:31:36.950 --> 00:31:39.300
and to show them what a
scientist can look like,

00:31:39.300 --> 00:31:40.770
what science is like

00:31:40.770 --> 00:31:43.210
so that they might see themselves in me

00:31:43.210 --> 00:31:45.710
and see themselves in the lab.

00:31:45.710 --> 00:31:47.850
I have high schoolers through post-docs

00:31:47.850 --> 00:31:50.070
that conduct research with me at NASA.

00:31:50.070 --> 00:31:53.060
And we're all learning
and growing together,

00:31:53.060 --> 00:31:56.000
gaining new skills and
advancing our careers.

00:31:56.000 --> 00:31:58.050
And I think it's really easy

00:31:58.050 --> 00:32:03.050
that for us to oversimplify
or not realize the gravity

00:32:03.200 --> 00:32:05.680
that just one touch or one interaction

00:32:05.680 --> 00:32:10.150
can have on a student as they
move through their education.

00:32:10.150 --> 00:32:11.610
And at one time in Yale,

00:32:11.610 --> 00:32:14.850
I gave a lab tour to some high schoolers

00:32:14.850 --> 00:32:16.300
and one of those high schoolers

00:32:16.300 --> 00:32:17.840
reached out to me on LinkedIn.

00:32:17.840 --> 00:32:21.108
And I'd say every like year and
a half, she sends a message.

00:32:21.108 --> 00:32:23.010
Lyndsey, you might not remember me,

00:32:23.010 --> 00:32:24.800
but you toured me around your lab

00:32:24.800 --> 00:32:27.000
and I'm going to grad school,

00:32:27.000 --> 00:32:28.720
and I'm in my third year,

00:32:28.720 --> 00:32:30.240
and I'm doing this.

00:32:30.240 --> 00:32:32.039
And I'm like, wow.

00:32:32.039 --> 00:32:34.980
So it's so valuable.

00:32:34.980 --> 00:32:36.230
And there are people who,

00:32:36.230 --> 00:32:38.080
it's like two trains passing in the night,

00:32:38.080 --> 00:32:40.530
but you make an imprint on them

00:32:40.530 --> 00:32:42.480
and you send them in a positive direction.

00:32:42.480 --> 00:32:44.420
I think we can all draw from that,

00:32:44.420 --> 00:32:46.630
like how Tarek and I were talking about.

00:32:46.630 --> 00:32:47.720
The hot and the cold.

00:32:47.720 --> 00:32:50.580
So when things aren't going so well,

00:32:50.580 --> 00:32:52.830
you draw from those positive interactions

00:32:52.830 --> 00:32:53.770
and that encouragement,

00:32:53.770 --> 00:32:55.700
and that can really help you get through

00:32:55.700 --> 00:32:57.630
to the next sunny spot.

00:32:57.630 --> 00:32:59.680
- I think it's so wonderful.

00:32:59.680 --> 00:33:02.360
The work that you do to
inspire high school students

00:33:02.360 --> 00:33:04.710
and it's a perfect segue into,

00:33:04.710 --> 00:33:05.730
and other students as well,

00:33:05.730 --> 00:33:07.520
but it's a perfect segue
into one of the questions

00:33:07.520 --> 00:33:10.280
that we received from one
of the attendees tonight.

00:33:10.280 --> 00:33:11.912
I'd like to jump to it.

00:33:11.912 --> 00:33:13.400
It's very specific,

00:33:13.400 --> 00:33:17.700
but this person is asking,
what simplified math project

00:33:17.700 --> 00:33:21.010
could be used to inspire
their middle school

00:33:21.010 --> 00:33:24.610
and high school students
to explore solar energy?

00:33:24.610 --> 00:33:27.370
- Oh, that's a good question, that's fun.

00:33:27.370 --> 00:33:30.330
So a couple of things come to mind.

00:33:30.330 --> 00:33:32.050
So since you're doing math,

00:33:32.050 --> 00:33:33.120
I think you can look at solar

00:33:33.120 --> 00:33:36.930
from a lot of different vantage points.

00:33:36.930 --> 00:33:39.640
You can look at the
economics of solar, right?

00:33:39.640 --> 00:33:41.960
How much does a panel cost?

00:33:41.960 --> 00:33:44.450
How long is it going to take before,

00:33:44.450 --> 00:33:47.890
maybe you're net positive
on this investment?

00:33:47.890 --> 00:33:50.990
Maybe you want to compare solar power

00:33:50.990 --> 00:33:55.240
over the lifetime of an
operating panel cradle to grave,

00:33:55.240 --> 00:33:59.480
to getting equivalent energy
from a coal power plant.

00:33:59.480 --> 00:34:03.010
So you can see what is
the environmental impact?

00:34:03.010 --> 00:34:05.383
What is the carbon footprint?

00:34:05.383 --> 00:34:07.800
What's the best route that you could take?

00:34:07.800 --> 00:34:09.100
I think you could look at things

00:34:09.100 --> 00:34:12.330
about the structure of your solar panels,

00:34:12.330 --> 00:34:17.170
how many panels will you
need to power a certain load

00:34:17.170 --> 00:34:18.950
and how much is it gonna weigh?

00:34:18.950 --> 00:34:20.100
How big is it going to be?

00:34:20.100 --> 00:34:22.230
How much areas it gonna take up?

00:34:22.230 --> 00:34:24.160
I think that would be really fun.

00:34:24.160 --> 00:34:25.857
So I can think of a few things,

00:34:25.857 --> 00:34:29.400
I also like to think of the
human aspect and things.

00:34:29.400 --> 00:34:32.220
So aside from cost and weight,

00:34:32.220 --> 00:34:36.920
you may be able to think
of developing nations

00:34:36.920 --> 00:34:40.250
or the impact of the climate on animals.

00:34:40.250 --> 00:34:42.830
And maybe there's some investigations

00:34:42.830 --> 00:34:45.030
that they could look
at there too for solar.

00:34:46.330 --> 00:34:47.163
- Thank you for that.

00:34:47.163 --> 00:34:49.070
That's really helpful.

00:34:49.070 --> 00:34:51.220
And Tarek you can jump in at anytime.

00:34:51.220 --> 00:34:53.710
We're getting questions
from the attendees tonight.

00:34:53.710 --> 00:34:57.370
So I want to kind of get to
those before our time runs out,

00:34:57.370 --> 00:35:01.100
but someone in the Q&amp;A asked,

00:35:01.100 --> 00:35:02.800
what was your most challenging moment

00:35:02.800 --> 00:35:05.520
or experience as a minority female student

00:35:05.520 --> 00:35:07.300
in the sciences at Yale?

00:35:07.300 --> 00:35:10.993
Or how did you meet or
overcome that challenge?

00:35:12.490 --> 00:35:14.440
- Yeah, I had a few challenging moments

00:35:16.780 --> 00:35:18.270
but they all had a common thread

00:35:18.270 --> 00:35:20.630
and the common thread in
these challenging moments

00:35:20.630 --> 00:35:25.630
was people in positions
of authority or power,

00:35:26.000 --> 00:35:28.320
or even sometimes my peers,

00:35:28.320 --> 00:35:31.320
were committing micro or
macro aggressions against me

00:35:31.320 --> 00:35:35.380
to make me feel like I don't belong.

00:35:35.380 --> 00:35:38.340
So that could look like someone coming

00:35:38.340 --> 00:35:39.790
into a lab that I'm in,

00:35:39.790 --> 00:35:41.910
even though you clearly have to be badged

00:35:41.910 --> 00:35:44.520
and trained to have access
to a piece of equipment

00:35:44.520 --> 00:35:46.730
and asking, "Are you a high-schooler,

00:35:46.730 --> 00:35:47.850
what are you doing here?

00:35:47.850 --> 00:35:49.850
How did you get here?"

00:35:49.850 --> 00:35:50.890
The person who asked me that

00:35:50.890 --> 00:35:53.410
was the same person who
trained me on the equipment

00:35:53.410 --> 00:35:56.411
that I was a user for.

00:35:56.411 --> 00:35:58.910
And that made me feel
really bad in that day.

00:35:58.910 --> 00:36:00.530
And it was also one of those days,

00:36:00.530 --> 00:36:02.410
Tarek, back to the highs and lows

00:36:02.410 --> 00:36:05.470
that nothing in my
experiment was going right

00:36:05.470 --> 00:36:06.650
and I didn't want to be there

00:36:06.650 --> 00:36:07.980
and I just wanted to be in bed.

00:36:07.980 --> 00:36:10.330
And it took everything to drag myself up

00:36:10.330 --> 00:36:12.410
and hear gums that like straw

00:36:12.410 --> 00:36:14.450
that breaks the camel's back for the day.

00:36:14.450 --> 00:36:16.990
So I got through those times

00:36:16.990 --> 00:36:19.480
by leaning on my support network.

00:36:19.480 --> 00:36:23.680
And that was my now husband Charles Brown,

00:36:23.680 --> 00:36:26.120
who then was a very friendly classmate

00:36:26.120 --> 00:36:28.280
that provided a lot of support.

00:36:28.280 --> 00:36:29.970
And some of my other friends

00:36:29.970 --> 00:36:32.750
that I'm still really close to today,

00:36:32.750 --> 00:36:34.150
and that was my parents.

00:36:34.150 --> 00:36:36.970
And those were my college professor,

00:36:36.970 --> 00:36:38.577
and you reach out and tell them,

00:36:38.577 --> 00:36:39.630
"This happened today

00:36:39.630 --> 00:36:41.020
and this is how I'm feeling."

00:36:41.020 --> 00:36:42.180
And they dust you off

00:36:42.180 --> 00:36:43.160
and they pick you up,

00:36:43.160 --> 00:36:45.370
and maybe they give you some chocolate,

00:36:45.370 --> 00:36:47.290
whatever it takes to recharge you

00:36:47.290 --> 00:36:48.700
and get you back going.

00:36:48.700 --> 00:36:51.550
So it's okay to feel those things

00:36:51.550 --> 00:36:55.010
and acknowledge them and take a pause.

00:36:55.010 --> 00:36:56.257
But I always reminded myself

00:36:56.257 --> 00:37:00.270
that no individual is
going to knock me off

00:37:00.270 --> 00:37:02.720
of my life's path.

00:37:02.720 --> 00:37:05.750
I didn't want to get taken
out like that, so to speak.

00:37:05.750 --> 00:37:09.543
And I refused to let someone win,

00:37:10.710 --> 00:37:14.600
cause it's clear that you must
not want me in this space,

00:37:14.600 --> 00:37:16.120
but I want to be here.

00:37:16.120 --> 00:37:18.710
So I'm going to fight to stay here.

00:37:18.710 --> 00:37:22.870
And I also began really
studying the humanities more

00:37:22.870 --> 00:37:25.920
and I read a lot of scholarship
about marginalized people

00:37:25.920 --> 00:37:29.540
because when you start to see
what the system looks like

00:37:29.540 --> 00:37:31.550
and that this is a large system,

00:37:31.550 --> 00:37:32.470
and that it might sound

00:37:32.470 --> 00:37:35.290
like an offhand comment
someone made to in math class,

00:37:35.290 --> 00:37:38.350
but really they're just
reinforcing these gender and race

00:37:38.350 --> 00:37:39.590
and societal things

00:37:39.590 --> 00:37:42.086
that have been going on
for hundreds of years.

00:37:42.086 --> 00:37:44.910
That knowledge in scholarship

00:37:44.910 --> 00:37:46.850
has made me a lot more equipped

00:37:46.850 --> 00:37:51.680
to identify and handle those
aggressions when I face them.

00:37:51.680 --> 00:37:53.770
- Thank you so much Lyndsey.

00:37:53.770 --> 00:37:56.020
You were talking a lot about your network

00:37:56.020 --> 00:37:58.710
and how important that support system was.

00:37:58.710 --> 00:38:01.410
We have a question from
one of our attendees

00:38:01.410 --> 00:38:04.100
who is actually, this
person is actually editing

00:38:04.100 --> 00:38:08.260
a collection of African-Americans
experiences at Yale.

00:38:08.260 --> 00:38:11.120
And is curious about your awareness

00:38:11.120 --> 00:38:15.430
or your association

00:38:15.430 --> 00:38:18.610
with the Afro American
Cultural Center at Yale.

00:38:18.610 --> 00:38:20.820
- Yes, I was very much aware of

00:38:20.820 --> 00:38:23.050
and affiliated with the house.

00:38:23.050 --> 00:38:26.730
I found community and
support in the house.

00:38:26.730 --> 00:38:29.490
I appreciated their programming

00:38:29.490 --> 00:38:32.320
and I had a lot of good times there.

00:38:32.320 --> 00:38:36.280
I also hosted some events in the house

00:38:36.280 --> 00:38:38.410
through the Yale League of Black Scientist

00:38:38.410 --> 00:38:43.200
and I was a member of the
Bouschet Honor Society

00:38:43.200 --> 00:38:44.330
and still I am.

00:38:44.330 --> 00:38:48.920
So that was a very helpful tool for me.

00:38:48.920 --> 00:38:50.890
That was where I met
some of my first friends,

00:38:50.890 --> 00:38:53.420
at the welcome barbecue.

00:38:53.420 --> 00:38:56.490
Very quickly you get the books,

00:38:56.490 --> 00:38:59.010
the mountain of textbooks
kind of hits you in the face

00:38:59.010 --> 00:39:01.360
and you get locked in the lab.

00:39:01.360 --> 00:39:02.800
And for me that was Mason lab,

00:39:02.800 --> 00:39:07.180
but I absolutely found
a lot of great community

00:39:07.180 --> 00:39:08.080
through the house.

00:39:10.320 --> 00:39:11.853
- Thank you for answering.

00:39:13.210 --> 00:39:14.580
Another question here.

00:39:14.580 --> 00:39:17.260
What is the culture
like regarding diversity

00:39:17.260 --> 00:39:19.263
and inclusion at NASA?

00:39:20.340 --> 00:39:23.760
- Yeah, so that's a great question.

00:39:23.760 --> 00:39:25.470
And the culture is pretty good.

00:39:25.470 --> 00:39:29.110
The government has actually
one of the leaders in diversity

00:39:29.110 --> 00:39:31.420
and we are typically,

00:39:31.420 --> 00:39:33.560
historically, the government
has been a great place

00:39:33.560 --> 00:39:35.770
for minoritized people

00:39:35.770 --> 00:39:40.170
to find success and employment,

00:39:40.170 --> 00:39:42.440
and have upward mobility.

00:39:42.440 --> 00:39:44.870
I think it's great that that's the case.

00:39:44.870 --> 00:39:47.250
I think it government would fail

00:39:47.250 --> 00:39:48.710
if it's federal employees

00:39:48.710 --> 00:39:50.440
were not an accurate representation

00:39:50.440 --> 00:39:53.010
of the constituents that it represents.

00:39:53.010 --> 00:39:55.610
So NASA is good with that.

00:39:55.610 --> 00:40:00.610
NASA also has affinity
groups and support networks,

00:40:01.300 --> 00:40:03.240
and they facilitate these relationships

00:40:03.240 --> 00:40:05.520
and we host listening sessions.

00:40:05.520 --> 00:40:09.320
And there is a lot of opportunity

00:40:09.320 --> 00:40:10.790
to be your authentic self

00:40:11.630 --> 00:40:14.850
and to engage with other
people that are like you.

00:40:14.850 --> 00:40:16.930
But also I feel very respected

00:40:16.930 --> 00:40:21.470
and included by all members,
all of my colleagues,

00:40:21.470 --> 00:40:23.230
which is tremendously important.

00:40:23.230 --> 00:40:24.490
And one of the other ways

00:40:24.490 --> 00:40:26.620
that I think is more subtle

00:40:26.620 --> 00:40:28.770
but it's tremendously impactful,

00:40:28.770 --> 00:40:33.770
is how much NASA values
and conducts outreach.

00:40:35.200 --> 00:40:37.200
And that is critical to the NASA mission.

00:40:37.200 --> 00:40:39.940
We do a lot of things for K through 12.

00:40:39.940 --> 00:40:40.920
We do a lot of things

00:40:40.920 --> 00:40:44.450
to bring underrepresented
students into science,

00:40:44.450 --> 00:40:45.870
get them excited about space

00:40:45.870 --> 00:40:46.880
and expose them to them.

00:40:46.880 --> 00:40:49.410
And I think that says a
lot about an organization

00:40:49.410 --> 00:40:51.210
or agency's values

00:40:51.210 --> 00:40:55.210
when they are doing the
work to fill that pipeline,

00:40:55.210 --> 00:40:56.970
to release some of those valves

00:40:56.970 --> 00:40:58.670
and to get everyone through.

00:40:58.670 --> 00:41:01.980
And NASA does that really
well across the board.

00:41:01.980 --> 00:41:02.813
- That's great.

00:41:02.813 --> 00:41:04.530
It sounds like NASA has
been a really great place

00:41:04.530 --> 00:41:08.320
for you to work and to serve also.

00:41:08.320 --> 00:41:09.250
We do have a question here,

00:41:09.250 --> 00:41:11.860
someone wants to know if
you've ever felt unqualified

00:41:11.860 --> 00:41:13.450
for the job or position you were in.

00:41:13.450 --> 00:41:16.020
So kind of hitting on imposter syndrome,

00:41:16.020 --> 00:41:18.060
how you were able to overcome self doubt,

00:41:18.060 --> 00:41:19.600
if you had any?

00:41:19.600 --> 00:41:21.363
- Oh, I've had self doubt for sure.

00:41:22.780 --> 00:41:23.700
And that's a tough one.

00:41:23.700 --> 00:41:27.410
And I think to deal with self doubt,

00:41:27.410 --> 00:41:31.990
I remember everything
that I've come through

00:41:31.990 --> 00:41:33.410
to get to this point.

00:41:33.410 --> 00:41:35.770
I've undergone a lot of training.

00:41:35.770 --> 00:41:38.490
I've been through a lot of school,

00:41:38.490 --> 00:41:41.340
I've faced a lot of challenges

00:41:41.340 --> 00:41:43.250
and I'm still standing, I'm still here.

00:41:43.250 --> 00:41:45.240
I find a way to get through all of it.

00:41:45.240 --> 00:41:48.160
And you didn't get through all
of these gates by accident.

00:41:48.160 --> 00:41:50.070
Nobody accidentally lay you in the college

00:41:50.070 --> 00:41:51.310
and you didn't accidentally graduate,

00:41:51.310 --> 00:41:52.550
and you accidentally get to grad school,

00:41:52.550 --> 00:41:54.010
and you accidentally get a job,

00:41:54.010 --> 00:41:56.700
like who has that kind of luck, right?

00:41:56.700 --> 00:41:58.490
So at a certain point,

00:41:58.490 --> 00:42:00.480
you really need to start to believe

00:42:00.480 --> 00:42:03.860
that you're not here by a clerical error,

00:42:03.860 --> 00:42:05.430
you earned your spot

00:42:05.430 --> 00:42:08.640
and then recall all the things

00:42:08.640 --> 00:42:11.440
that previously seemed impossible

00:42:11.440 --> 00:42:13.470
that you managed to do

00:42:13.470 --> 00:42:16.030
and start to own who you are.

00:42:16.030 --> 00:42:19.220
I still have self-doubt,
I still get nerves

00:42:19.220 --> 00:42:22.560
or you see this daunting
task in front of you.

00:42:22.560 --> 00:42:23.880
And also for that,

00:42:23.880 --> 00:42:28.250
my family and my peers that I
went through grad school with,

00:42:28.250 --> 00:42:29.730
we hype each other up

00:42:29.730 --> 00:42:31.680
and we're not hyping
each other up with lies.

00:42:31.680 --> 00:42:35.070
I feel like it's so much easier
for someone external to you

00:42:35.070 --> 00:42:36.560
to see your greatness.

00:42:36.560 --> 00:42:38.560
So if my friend calls me from grad school

00:42:38.560 --> 00:42:39.700
and I was like, "Oh, I don't know how

00:42:39.700 --> 00:42:40.533
I'm going to do this."

00:42:40.533 --> 00:42:42.000
I'm like, "Girl, are you kidding me?

00:42:42.000 --> 00:42:43.830
Do you remember that you did this

00:42:43.830 --> 00:42:45.020
and this and this,

00:42:45.020 --> 00:42:46.090
and he didn't even help you

00:42:46.090 --> 00:42:47.760
and you did this and this."

00:42:47.760 --> 00:42:51.230
So it's so easy to see the
greatness in someone else.

00:42:51.230 --> 00:42:53.610
So let your friends and
your support network

00:42:53.610 --> 00:42:56.990
and your family show a mirror,
hold a mirror up to you

00:42:56.990 --> 00:42:59.210
and show you your
greatness and hype you up

00:42:59.210 --> 00:43:00.260
because you've done a lot

00:43:00.260 --> 00:43:02.430
and you will do so much more.

00:43:02.430 --> 00:43:03.263
- Thank you for that.

00:43:03.263 --> 00:43:06.400
I know that answer probably
helped a lot of people

00:43:06.400 --> 00:43:08.120
who struggled with imposter syndrome.

00:43:08.120 --> 00:43:08.973
So thank you.

00:43:09.850 --> 00:43:12.680
There's another question here
from one of the attendees

00:43:12.680 --> 00:43:14.080
and I am not a scientist,

00:43:14.080 --> 00:43:15.130
so I'm just gonna read it

00:43:15.130 --> 00:43:17.720
and let you answer as you will,

00:43:17.720 --> 00:43:19.250
but the question is,

00:43:19.250 --> 00:43:23.170
what is your opinion on
single Adam catalyst?

00:43:23.170 --> 00:43:24.640
Do you think that it's possible

00:43:24.640 --> 00:43:28.053
to replace nanoparticle
catalyst in the future?

00:43:29.270 --> 00:43:32.040
- So I'm actually not
an expert in catalyst,

00:43:32.040 --> 00:43:35.470
so I don't have that much to say about it

00:43:35.470 --> 00:43:37.283
but I will say in general,

00:43:39.001 --> 00:43:41.300
I am a fan of all technology

00:43:41.300 --> 00:43:44.220
and all novel methods to solve problems.

00:43:44.220 --> 00:43:48.079
One of my least favorite
things about science,

00:43:48.079 --> 00:43:53.079
is that people have these
large sweeping opinions

00:43:53.150 --> 00:43:55.500
about what is worthwhile to investigate

00:43:55.500 --> 00:43:59.110
and what is trivial and
what isn't that exciting.

00:43:59.110 --> 00:44:02.430
And I think that that
is a show of arrogance

00:44:02.430 --> 00:44:04.310
and oftentimes ignorance.

00:44:04.310 --> 00:44:06.961
I find merit in any discovery.

00:44:06.961 --> 00:44:09.920
Gratefully when I was trained up at NASA,

00:44:09.920 --> 00:44:13.260
one thing they taught me was
a null result is a result.

00:44:13.260 --> 00:44:14.770
We write it up and we file it

00:44:14.770 --> 00:44:16.030
because if you can tell someone

00:44:16.030 --> 00:44:17.860
that this route is a dead end,

00:44:17.860 --> 00:44:20.020
that is knowledge that
we as a species need,

00:44:20.020 --> 00:44:23.823
we don't need to keep
reinventing the wheel.

00:44:23.823 --> 00:44:27.260
So I am a fan of exploring
all avenues of science

00:44:27.260 --> 00:44:29.550
and I really wish that funding agencies

00:44:29.550 --> 00:44:31.700
and people who make those policy

00:44:31.700 --> 00:44:33.580
and decision makers also felt

00:44:33.580 --> 00:44:35.030
that everything is worthwhile.

00:44:35.030 --> 00:44:37.523
So single catalysts, go for it.

00:44:39.060 --> 00:44:39.893
- Thank you.

00:44:44.750 --> 00:44:46.283
Tarek, were you gonna unmute?

00:44:46.283 --> 00:44:47.525
I thought maybe you had a question.

00:44:47.525 --> 00:44:49.527
- Yeah, yeah, I was about to,

00:44:49.527 --> 00:44:51.611
no, thank you, this is great.

00:44:51.611 --> 00:44:53.500
I keep having this recurring theme

00:44:53.500 --> 00:44:56.550
of how Lyndsey understands a lot of times

00:44:56.550 --> 00:44:59.130
that a lot of people
will try to put science

00:44:59.130 --> 00:45:00.810
in terms of how it impacts society.

00:45:00.810 --> 00:45:01.643
And that's good.

00:45:01.643 --> 00:45:03.000
And we look at translation,

00:45:03.000 --> 00:45:04.747
we look at a lot of things like that,

00:45:04.747 --> 00:45:07.490
but the one thing that Lyndsey
reminds us a lot of times,

00:45:07.490 --> 00:45:08.560
is that you have to think about

00:45:08.560 --> 00:45:10.470
how society impacts your science

00:45:10.470 --> 00:45:15.310
and whether it is at the technology level

00:45:15.310 --> 00:45:16.690
through holistic approaches,

00:45:16.690 --> 00:45:18.810
or it is at the human level.

00:45:18.810 --> 00:45:21.280
And talking about an active effort

00:45:21.280 --> 00:45:25.900
to figure out how to engage with everyone

00:45:25.900 --> 00:45:27.800
and make sure that everyone's got a chance

00:45:27.800 --> 00:45:29.130
to learn about science.

00:45:29.130 --> 00:45:31.040
It is an active effort

00:45:31.040 --> 00:45:33.690
and this is something you
have to do with intent.

00:45:33.690 --> 00:45:35.900
And it's great to hear
all about the stories

00:45:35.900 --> 00:45:37.797
you've done in and out NASA.

00:45:37.797 --> 00:45:40.950
The one thing, as you know
I spent a few years in DC

00:45:41.830 --> 00:45:44.072
supporting the federal government

00:45:44.072 --> 00:45:46.020
and in nanoscience and
in emerging technologies,

00:45:46.020 --> 00:45:47.700
and I just have a true appreciation

00:45:47.700 --> 00:45:48.533
for the fact also

00:45:48.533 --> 00:45:50.690
that you probably coordinate all across

00:45:50.690 --> 00:45:53.100
with other agencies,
not just at NASA, right?

00:45:53.100 --> 00:45:56.010
Trying to make sure you consider efforts

00:45:56.010 --> 00:45:57.280
and do all that.

00:45:57.280 --> 00:46:00.160
And one of the questions we got,

00:46:00.160 --> 00:46:03.220
was what are the advantage
of working as a PI at NASA

00:46:03.220 --> 00:46:04.870
rather than a private company?

00:46:04.870 --> 00:46:06.050
I think there's a lot

00:46:06.050 --> 00:46:07.250
and I let you answer it,

00:46:07.250 --> 00:46:09.620
but there's this global aspect,

00:46:09.620 --> 00:46:11.390
you you're working in an agency

00:46:11.390 --> 00:46:15.610
that's looking at the stars at earth.

00:46:15.610 --> 00:46:18.430
I mean, it's so large and so big,

00:46:18.430 --> 00:46:20.310
but I'll let you answer it.

00:46:20.310 --> 00:46:21.220
- Yeah, thank you.

00:46:21.220 --> 00:46:22.630
That's a really good question.

00:46:22.630 --> 00:46:25.800
So I think there are so many benefits

00:46:25.800 --> 00:46:29.080
and I'll share with you
guys some of my favorites.

00:46:29.080 --> 00:46:33.020
So one of my favorite things about NASA,

00:46:33.020 --> 00:46:38.020
is that we don't do research for profit

00:46:39.150 --> 00:46:43.150
and a lot of private places

00:46:43.150 --> 00:46:46.170
at the base of research and development,

00:46:46.170 --> 00:46:50.740
they're looking at what
will the output of this be?

00:46:50.740 --> 00:46:51.800
What can we sell?

00:46:51.800 --> 00:46:53.390
How will we make money?

00:46:53.390 --> 00:46:56.150
And it's very rare to find opportunities

00:46:56.150 --> 00:46:57.810
where you can conduct research

00:46:57.810 --> 00:47:00.700
that doesn't have to immediately show

00:47:00.700 --> 00:47:03.440
how it's going to make money.

00:47:03.440 --> 00:47:05.340
So what that results in,

00:47:05.340 --> 00:47:10.060
is that the NASA funds
it's scientists like me

00:47:10.060 --> 00:47:13.910
to do research that is
seen in the private sector

00:47:13.910 --> 00:47:15.800
as too high risk,

00:47:15.800 --> 00:47:17.730
because they don't know
if it will pan out,

00:47:17.730 --> 00:47:22.090
they don't quite see the
vision 20, 30 years out.

00:47:22.090 --> 00:47:25.690
So I am best at the cutting edge

00:47:25.690 --> 00:47:28.280
and we get to be tremendously imaginative

00:47:28.280 --> 00:47:29.840
and we get to think of things like

00:47:29.840 --> 00:47:32.690
what if we printed
solar cells on the moon?

00:47:32.690 --> 00:47:34.510
And it's so fun.

00:47:34.510 --> 00:47:37.650
So I really love that opportunity

00:47:37.650 --> 00:47:41.100
that I think is unique to
being at a government agency

00:47:41.100 --> 00:47:43.480
or specifically at NASA.

00:47:43.480 --> 00:47:46.130
Something else, NASA is
working on space travel,

00:47:46.130 --> 00:47:48.730
and I find that tremendously exciting.

00:47:48.730 --> 00:47:52.250
And the way that we conduct
research at NASA is at first,

00:47:52.250 --> 00:47:53.960
there is something
called a decadal survey.

00:47:53.960 --> 00:47:56.650
So every 10 years, people sit down

00:47:56.650 --> 00:47:58.280
and they think 10 years out.

00:47:58.280 --> 00:48:01.430
And the discussion is, where, what space,

00:48:01.430 --> 00:48:02.850
what science missions.

00:48:02.850 --> 00:48:03.890
So like what planets

00:48:03.890 --> 00:48:07.240
or bodies would they like
to visit in 10 years?

00:48:07.240 --> 00:48:08.820
And they'll say, "Oh,
it would be really cool

00:48:08.820 --> 00:48:10.270
if we could go to (indistinct)

00:48:10.270 --> 00:48:13.070
or let's try to set up camp on the moon."

00:48:13.070 --> 00:48:15.460
And then from that we divorced,

00:48:15.460 --> 00:48:18.390
what technology do we need to invent,

00:48:18.390 --> 00:48:20.670
so that we can fulfill this mission.

00:48:20.670 --> 00:48:23.040
And that's where the scientists like me,

00:48:23.040 --> 00:48:24.490
the engineers come in,

00:48:24.490 --> 00:48:26.020
is to create this technology

00:48:26.020 --> 00:48:28.160
such that we can conduct those missions.

00:48:28.160 --> 00:48:32.760
So we're kind of on demand,
enabling this technology.

00:48:32.760 --> 00:48:34.610
And then there are so
many spinoff technologies

00:48:34.610 --> 00:48:36.710
that we get from that.

00:48:36.710 --> 00:48:39.290
As we develop these things for space,

00:48:39.290 --> 00:48:43.210
we find a lot of practical
uses for them here on earth,

00:48:43.210 --> 00:48:45.850
and then we spin them out again

00:48:45.850 --> 00:48:48.470
to companies that end
up boosting the economy

00:48:48.470 --> 00:48:51.530
and giving us this great new technology

00:48:51.530 --> 00:48:54.570
that probably would not
have been discovered

00:48:54.570 --> 00:48:57.090
if we didn't have that latitude

00:48:57.090 --> 00:48:58.940
and that freedom of imagination

00:48:58.940 --> 00:49:01.890
to think so wonky and
so far out of the box

00:49:01.890 --> 00:49:04.170
and to be so creative.

00:49:04.170 --> 00:49:08.030
And I also love that NASA
really values people,

00:49:08.030 --> 00:49:09.820
the environment and the communities.

00:49:09.820 --> 00:49:12.600
So it it's tremendously
important to me as a person

00:49:12.600 --> 00:49:16.810
that I am doing little
to no harm to our earth.

00:49:16.810 --> 00:49:18.830
And I appreciate working at a place

00:49:18.830 --> 00:49:23.830
that's not polluting,
not robbing resources

00:49:24.410 --> 00:49:28.930
and not being a negative
presence on our planet.

00:49:28.930 --> 00:49:33.303
So I really appreciate just
the values of our agency.

00:49:34.310 --> 00:49:35.550
- This is great.

00:49:35.550 --> 00:49:37.870
What I've been hearing also,

00:49:37.870 --> 00:49:40.830
I think that resonates
actually in other disciplines,

00:49:40.830 --> 00:49:43.140
I feel like, it goes back to that question

00:49:43.140 --> 00:49:44.377
about the (indistinct).

00:49:45.970 --> 00:49:48.800
I mean, a lot of times you
want to think about the problem

00:49:48.800 --> 00:49:50.870
and then think about
the challenge in general

00:49:50.870 --> 00:49:52.340
and think about what makes sense,

00:49:52.340 --> 00:49:55.420
just like what you do in
biotechnology for example,

00:49:55.420 --> 00:49:57.640
what does the biology tell you

00:49:57.640 --> 00:49:59.350
and then figure out what makes sense

00:49:59.350 --> 00:50:01.080
in terms of what
technologies you want develop

00:50:01.080 --> 00:50:02.640
to treat this disease?

00:50:02.640 --> 00:50:07.293
And I think, you don't try to
push a particular platform.

00:50:07.293 --> 00:50:09.980
It's just for the sake
of it being cool, right?

00:50:09.980 --> 00:50:13.353
And so I love that, I'm
also hearing the fact that,

00:50:14.200 --> 00:50:16.250
your passion for the environment

00:50:16.250 --> 00:50:18.270
and thinking about policy and everything,

00:50:18.270 --> 00:50:20.220
I mean, I can imagine for example,

00:50:20.220 --> 00:50:23.330
that taking a step back,
you're probably engaging,

00:50:23.330 --> 00:50:25.456
for example, EPA or other.

00:50:25.456 --> 00:50:27.480
Did you ever have an instance

00:50:27.480 --> 00:50:31.240
when you were like you're
actually affecting policy

00:50:31.240 --> 00:50:32.213
at a higher level?

00:50:33.400 --> 00:50:37.410
- Yeah, so I think that's
something also I appreciate

00:50:37.410 --> 00:50:38.670
about being with the government,

00:50:38.670 --> 00:50:41.590
is we are the eyes and ears

00:50:41.590 --> 00:50:43.440
and the checkpoints of the government.

00:50:43.440 --> 00:50:45.531
So we're the subject matter experts

00:50:45.531 --> 00:50:49.520
that our leaders are,

00:50:49.520 --> 00:50:51.100
congressional leaders and our lawmakers

00:50:51.100 --> 00:50:54.708
and our policymakers rely
on to say, "Is this good?

00:50:54.708 --> 00:50:55.990
Should we be doing this?

00:50:55.990 --> 00:50:57.180
Maybe we should not be doing this,

00:50:57.180 --> 00:50:58.280
should we fund this more?

00:50:58.280 --> 00:51:01.490
Is this what direction
should we be going in?"

00:51:01.490 --> 00:51:04.640
So I appreciate being in that position.

00:51:04.640 --> 00:51:06.500
And although I can say those are wings

00:51:06.500 --> 00:51:07.910
that I'm still growing

00:51:07.910 --> 00:51:12.070
and I haven't entirely
taken off on that yet,

00:51:12.070 --> 00:51:13.770
that's something that I'm really excited

00:51:13.770 --> 00:51:14.880
and passionate about

00:51:14.880 --> 00:51:18.010
because I think these
decisions need to be made

00:51:18.010 --> 00:51:23.010
thinking about the long-term
longevity of our species.

00:51:24.460 --> 00:51:27.057
We need to be thinking about everyone,

00:51:27.057 --> 00:51:30.840
any technology, any large
decisions or directions we take,

00:51:30.840 --> 00:51:33.590
need to benefit all people.

00:51:33.590 --> 00:51:34.850
So these are some of the things

00:51:34.850 --> 00:51:36.290
that I'm really interested in,

00:51:36.290 --> 00:51:40.170
and there are opportunities
for that cross-pollination.

00:51:40.170 --> 00:51:42.920
NASA is sometimes seen
as the wonkier folks

00:51:42.920 --> 00:51:46.780
when we do sit down cause
we're with the air force, army,

00:51:46.780 --> 00:51:48.280
and the Navy

00:51:48.280 --> 00:51:50.120
and they have their
viewpoint of the world.

00:51:50.120 --> 00:51:51.360
And we're literally over here,

00:51:51.360 --> 00:51:53.950
like, let's just hold hands,

00:51:53.950 --> 00:51:54.783
wouldn't it be cool if,

00:51:54.783 --> 00:51:58.467
so we're a bit of the
flower child in that way,

00:51:58.467 --> 00:52:01.490
but I think we're really
valuable in that sense.

00:52:01.490 --> 00:52:02.390
- That's great, thank you.

00:52:02.390 --> 00:52:06.400
We actually have a really,
really fun question to hear

00:52:06.400 --> 00:52:08.603
from the Colorado Yale Association.

00:52:09.570 --> 00:52:11.510
Have you watched the new "Dune" movie

00:52:11.510 --> 00:52:15.891
and you like science
fiction or Afro futurism?

00:52:15.891 --> 00:52:17.603
I love that movie by the way.

00:52:17.603 --> 00:52:18.436
- And I watch which movie?

00:52:18.436 --> 00:52:19.269
- [Tarek] The New Dune.

00:52:20.790 --> 00:52:21.960
- Yes, I did watch that.

00:52:21.960 --> 00:52:23.440
And that was fun.

00:52:23.440 --> 00:52:26.130
I've really been getting
into sci-fi lately.

00:52:26.130 --> 00:52:29.150
I've been reading it a bit
more than watching movies

00:52:29.150 --> 00:52:31.210
cause that's a bit more my speed,

00:52:31.210 --> 00:52:33.450
but yeah, I enjoyed that movie

00:52:33.450 --> 00:52:36.330
and I also love sci-fi

00:52:36.330 --> 00:52:39.700
and I get really excited by sci-fi,

00:52:39.700 --> 00:52:44.160
they provide a lot of like
information and perspectives

00:52:44.160 --> 00:52:48.190
and kind of situations that
I hadn't considered yet.

00:52:48.190 --> 00:52:50.980
And then I'm like, "Could this happen,

00:52:50.980 --> 00:52:51.813
is this real?"

00:52:51.813 --> 00:52:53.506
Like maybe, so then I'm amped up

00:52:53.506 --> 00:52:54.339
and I want to go into the lab

00:52:54.339 --> 00:52:56.260
and try to make it happen.

00:52:56.260 --> 00:52:57.883
So, yeah, I'm a big fan.

00:52:59.220 --> 00:53:00.053
- That's really cool.

00:53:00.053 --> 00:53:00.940
Yeah, I'm a big fan of the movie.

00:53:00.940 --> 00:53:04.591
I really just, I was on
my seat the whole time

00:53:04.591 --> 00:53:05.785
and that was just wild.

00:53:05.785 --> 00:53:06.853
It was great.

00:53:06.853 --> 00:53:09.150
All right, well, I don't know Danica,

00:53:09.150 --> 00:53:12.583
do you think we have room,
maybe like one last question?

00:53:12.583 --> 00:53:14.210
- Yeah, I think maybe
for one last question,

00:53:14.210 --> 00:53:15.930
Lyndsey, thank you for that answer.

00:53:15.930 --> 00:53:19.090
I must admit, I do not know
that popular cultural reference,

00:53:19.090 --> 00:53:20.730
so I'm going to have
to catch up on "Dune."

00:53:20.730 --> 00:53:22.783
Sounds like something I should be into,

00:53:23.890 --> 00:53:26.500
but just for one you
closing question here,

00:53:26.500 --> 00:53:27.333
it's a big one,

00:53:27.333 --> 00:53:31.410
but I'm hoping that you'll
have a succinct answer.

00:53:31.410 --> 00:53:33.700
What is the one piece of advice

00:53:33.700 --> 00:53:36.790
you would give to Yale
students in the audience?

00:53:36.790 --> 00:53:38.040
- Oh, that's a great one.

00:53:39.630 --> 00:53:40.690
I'm going to cheat

00:53:40.690 --> 00:53:42.225
and give one personal advice

00:53:42.225 --> 00:53:44.070
and one professional advice.

00:53:44.070 --> 00:53:47.690
So the personal advice I
would tell Yale students is,

00:53:47.690 --> 00:53:51.510
remember that life goes on
while you're in grad school.

00:53:51.510 --> 00:53:55.970
So this is like four to
seven plus 10 ish years

00:53:55.970 --> 00:53:57.610
that you're there,

00:53:57.610 --> 00:53:59.200
but time isn't going to stand still.

00:53:59.200 --> 00:54:02.320
So nurture relationships with
your family and your friends,

00:54:02.320 --> 00:54:03.780
and be sure you take time for them

00:54:03.780 --> 00:54:05.410
because they're aging,

00:54:05.410 --> 00:54:07.990
they're moving, they're
going through life.

00:54:07.990 --> 00:54:10.900
And those milestones that
we experience as people

00:54:10.900 --> 00:54:13.260
are tremendously valuable.

00:54:13.260 --> 00:54:15.573
Professionally, I would say listen.

00:54:16.440 --> 00:54:18.010
Listen to things that you don't think

00:54:18.010 --> 00:54:20.650
are necessarily relevant
to your field of study

00:54:20.650 --> 00:54:23.160
or your goals, or your
career, or your life.

00:54:23.160 --> 00:54:24.510
There's just so much to learn

00:54:24.510 --> 00:54:25.890
from every discipline

00:54:26.868 --> 00:54:29.000
and the world we live in is
tremendously interdisciplinary.

00:54:29.000 --> 00:54:32.760
So I pull from various fields
in science in my research

00:54:32.760 --> 00:54:35.250
and humanities scholarship continues

00:54:35.250 --> 00:54:38.600
to provide me with a roadmap
to life as I navigate through.

00:54:38.600 --> 00:54:40.370
So be sure you listen a lot

00:54:40.370 --> 00:54:42.220
and have fun and learn something new.

00:54:43.326 --> 00:54:45.000
- Thank you so much Lyndsey.

00:54:45.000 --> 00:54:47.050
I think I can speak for everyone and say,

00:54:47.050 --> 00:54:48.973
this has been delightful.

00:54:50.260 --> 00:54:51.460
Everything you've said today,

00:54:51.460 --> 00:54:54.140
I think has been so impactful

00:54:54.140 --> 00:54:58.860
and I'm taking personally away
a lot from this conversation.

00:54:58.860 --> 00:55:02.070
So we just appreciate
you giving of your time

00:55:02.070 --> 00:55:05.730
and being so generous with your research

00:55:05.730 --> 00:55:09.300
and your sort of mentorship

00:55:09.300 --> 00:55:10.790
and outreach initiatives,

00:55:10.790 --> 00:55:11.890
and sort of helping us

00:55:11.890 --> 00:55:15.277
to really understand how
to create spaces for us

00:55:15.277 --> 00:55:17.850
and for students to create spaces,

00:55:17.850 --> 00:55:19.880
especially that are nurturing

00:55:19.880 --> 00:55:23.430
and will help them thrive
while here at Yale.

00:55:23.430 --> 00:55:26.010
I also want to thank Lynn Cooley.

00:55:26.010 --> 00:55:26.910
I wanna thank Tarek

00:55:26.910 --> 00:55:29.980
for being such a great
co-moderator with me

00:55:29.980 --> 00:55:32.450
and thank you all for
the questions submitted.

00:55:32.450 --> 00:55:33.640
It made for a very lively

00:55:33.640 --> 00:55:35.620
and important conversation.

00:55:35.620 --> 00:55:38.170
To all the students and
alumni who tuned in tonight,

00:55:38.170 --> 00:55:39.980
I hope you've enjoyed the discussion

00:55:39.980 --> 00:55:43.460
and will continue to help us
build a stronger community

00:55:43.460 --> 00:55:48.280
among graduate students,
faculty, and alumni at Yale.

00:55:48.280 --> 00:55:51.740
I'd also like to invite you
to the graduate schools,

